NetHack uses pointer arithmetic in several places. As NetHack is coded 
in C it has to use pointer arithmetic to handle variable sized 
structures. Both the monster and object structures can have more than 
one variable length part, so the address of the second has to be 
calculated by adding the length of the first to the address of the 
first. In an OO-language this address manipulation is hided by the compiler.

NetHack source uses pointers and indexes to arrays and have even 
conversion functions so that a pointer to a monster description array 
can be changed into an index to the same array (the inverse function is 
just pointer arithmetic). Why NetHack uses pointers to a static arrays I 
cannot say.

NetHack is a very good example how special functionality can be achieved 
in C. The whole concept of monsters having special abilities (pets, 
shopkeepers, vault guards, priests etc.) or objects sharing the same 
morphology (What do you get if you cast stone to flesh on a statue of 
your quest leader?) begs for an OO-language for implementation. But 
NetHack implemets its own OO where-ever it is needed and thus tries to 
conform to the widest audience (in sense of OS and compilers).

